1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,540
Okay, moving on to cerebrovascular disease.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:07,460
This is a neurologic deficit that arises due to cerebrovascular compromise.

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:11,660
It's usually due to ischemia, or a decreased blood flow to the brain,

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:14,380
but it can also be due to hemorrhage into the brain.

5
00:00:14,860 --> 00:00:17,460
Now, the first example would be global cerebral ischemia,

6
00:00:17,700 --> 00:00:20,040
but before we go there, why don't we just highlight a paradigm.

7
00:00:20,520 --> 00:00:24,540
So here's cerebrovascular disease, and of course it can be due to ischemia,

8
00:00:24,680 --> 00:00:25,980
or it can be due to hemorrhage.

9
00:00:25,980 --> 00:00:28,880
Now, when you have ischemia, that ischemia can be focal,

10
00:00:28,880 --> 00:00:30,680
or it can be global.

11
00:00:31,040 --> 00:00:33,700
And by focal, we mean that it involves a region of the brain,

12
00:00:33,940 --> 00:00:37,260
and by global, we mean that it diffusely involves the entire brain.

13
00:00:37,620 --> 00:00:40,640
And so global is called global cerebral ischemia,

14
00:00:40,920 --> 00:00:42,800
and that's what your text is referring to here.

15
00:00:43,180 --> 00:00:45,120
Now, how would you get global cerebral ischemia?

16
00:00:45,240 --> 00:00:47,520
Well, you could get it with low perfusion to the brain.

17
00:00:47,860 --> 00:00:51,860
For example, if you have atherosclerosis of a large vessel that's going into the brain,

18
00:00:52,220 --> 00:00:54,840
this would then decrease the amount of blood going into the brain,

19
00:00:54,840 --> 00:00:56,860
giving you a global cerebral ischemia.

20
00:00:56,860 --> 00:01:00,940
You could also see it with acute decrease in blood flow, for example, with shock.

21
00:01:01,280 --> 00:01:04,460
This would then reduce the overall amount of blood going into the brain.

22
00:01:04,900 --> 00:01:06,840
Similarly, we could see it with anemia.

23
00:01:07,360 --> 00:01:11,800
Again, with anemia, we'd have less oxygen going into the brain globally.

24
00:01:12,420 --> 00:01:17,020
And finally, in very high yield, you can see it with repeated episodes of hypoglycemia.

25
00:01:17,420 --> 00:01:20,640
Recall that glucose is an essential energy source for the brain.

26
00:01:20,640 --> 00:01:25,100
And so if you get hypoglycemia, that will globally affect the entire brain,

27
00:01:25,480 --> 00:01:27,420
creating a global cerebral ischemia.

28
00:01:28,200 --> 00:01:32,380
Now, the clinical features of global cerebral ischemia depend on the severity of the ischemia.

29
00:01:32,620 --> 00:01:35,220
So it can be mild, moderate, or severe.

30
00:01:35,600 --> 00:01:38,900
When it's mild, you get transient confusion with a complete recovery.

31
00:01:39,200 --> 00:01:41,160
The classic example would be an insulinoma.

32
00:01:41,360 --> 00:01:45,180
When patients have an insulinoma, the blood sugar goes down, they get confusion,

33
00:01:45,180 --> 00:01:48,200
you give them back glucose, and the confusion resolves.

34
00:01:48,460 --> 00:01:50,920
So that's an example of mild global ischemia.

35
00:01:51,320 --> 00:01:55,600
In severe global ischemia, you get diffuse necrosis of the brain due to the ischemia,

36
00:01:55,980 --> 00:01:57,760
and the patient probably wouldn't survive.

37
00:01:58,100 --> 00:02:01,160
However, if they did survive, they'd go on to a vegetative state.

38
00:02:01,620 --> 00:02:04,700
With moderate global ischemia, you get infarcts in watershed areas.

39
00:02:05,040 --> 00:02:09,000
The watershed areas are those regions that are fed by the very end of a circulation.

40
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:13,840
So, for example, the region fed by the very end of the anterior cerebral artery,

41
00:02:14,060 --> 00:02:16,380
or the very end of the middle cerebral artery.

42
00:02:16,740 --> 00:02:21,760
So these are watershed areas, and we can get infarctions in these areas with moderate global ischemia.

43
00:02:22,200 --> 00:02:25,300
In addition, we can get damage to highly vulnerable regions.

44
00:02:25,680 --> 00:02:28,000
For example, the pyramidal neurons of the cortex.

45
00:02:28,500 --> 00:02:31,840
These are neurons present in layers 3, 5, and 6 of the cortex.

46
00:02:32,160 --> 00:02:34,920
Recall that the cortex on histology has multiple layers,

47
00:02:34,920 --> 00:02:38,880
and we call them pyramidal neurons because they have a triangular appearance.

48
00:02:39,280 --> 00:02:41,920
Now imagine if this is the layers of the cortex,

49
00:02:42,440 --> 00:02:46,900
and we get damage to layers 3, layers 5, and layers 6,

50
00:02:47,160 --> 00:02:49,920
then this is going to create a line of necrosis,

51
00:02:50,260 --> 00:02:53,820
and so this is also known as cortical laminar necrosis.

52
00:02:54,160 --> 00:02:56,940
And board examiners like to go after this, so you should be aware of it.

53
00:02:57,300 --> 00:02:59,340
Cortical laminar necrosis.

54
00:02:59,840 --> 00:03:04,420
Now in addition, another vulnerable region would be the pyramidal neurons of the hippocampus.

55
00:03:04,420 --> 00:03:07,040
Remember that the hippocampus is in the temporal lobe,

56
00:03:07,040 --> 00:03:10,820
and its job is to transfer information to your long-term memory.

57
00:03:11,300 --> 00:03:13,220
And so with moderate global ischemia,

58
00:03:13,340 --> 00:03:16,760
you can also get damage to the pyramidal neurons of the hippocampus.

59
00:03:17,180 --> 00:03:21,120
And the third area that is highly vulnerable is the Purkinje layer of the cerebellum.

60
00:03:21,360 --> 00:03:24,880
So with moderate global ischemia, you could also get cerebellar signs.

61
00:03:25,620 --> 00:03:28,540
Moving on to ischemic stroke, let's go back to our paradigm.

62
00:03:28,680 --> 00:03:30,040
Here's cerebrovascular disease.

63
00:03:30,380 --> 00:03:32,960
Remember that it can be due to ischemia or hemorrhage,

64
00:03:32,960 --> 00:03:35,700
and when it's due to ischemia, that can be global or focal.

65
00:03:36,140 --> 00:03:40,660
Now when you have focal ischemia, that again involves one region of the brain,

66
00:03:40,940 --> 00:03:43,440
it then creates focal neurologic deficits.

67
00:03:43,940 --> 00:03:47,520
If the focal neurologic deficits last less than 24 hours,

68
00:03:48,040 --> 00:03:51,260
then we call that a transient ischemic attack.

69
00:03:51,780 --> 00:03:56,280
And if it lasts greater than 24 hours, then we call that an ischemic stroke.

70
00:03:56,280 --> 00:04:01,380
And so that's what your text is saying here when it says that ischemic stroke is regional ischemia

71
00:04:01,380 --> 00:04:03,880
that results in focal neurologic deficits.

72
00:04:04,280 --> 00:04:06,520
If it's greater than 24 hours, you call it a stroke.

73
00:04:06,720 --> 00:04:10,580
And if it's less than 24 hours, you call it a transient ischemic attack.

74
00:04:10,940 --> 00:04:15,440
Focusing on ischemic stroke, there are three ways by which you can develop an ischemic stroke.

75
00:04:15,660 --> 00:04:18,940
The first is thrombotic.

76
00:04:19,220 --> 00:04:22,040
And the idea here is that you've got a blood vessel feeding the cortex,

77
00:04:22,040 --> 00:04:26,280
and here's the cortex out here, you've got atherosclerosis within the blood vessel.

78
00:04:26,680 --> 00:04:28,440
This atherosclerotic plaque ruptures.

79
00:04:28,860 --> 00:04:32,460
That exposes subendothelial collagen and the necrotic debris within the plaque,

80
00:04:32,740 --> 00:04:34,520
resulting in formation of a thrombus.

81
00:04:34,780 --> 00:04:36,080
So now you cut the blood supply.

82
00:04:36,420 --> 00:04:40,780
When you cut this blood supply, you're then going to get infarction way out in the periphery of the cortex,

83
00:04:41,240 --> 00:04:45,120
and this infarction will be a pale infarction.

84
00:04:45,980 --> 00:04:49,480
Now the second type of stroke will be an embolic stroke.

85
00:04:49,480 --> 00:04:56,040
And the idea here is that you've got your blood vessel feeding the cortex.

86
00:04:56,520 --> 00:05:01,720
A thromboembolus, for example, from the left heart, comes in and lodges within this blood vessel.

87
00:05:01,900 --> 00:05:04,920
That cuts the blood supply long enough for the neurons to die,

88
00:05:05,300 --> 00:05:09,640
but then this thrombus gets lysed, and then blood goes back in,

89
00:05:09,920 --> 00:05:15,800
which then results in a hemorrhagic infarction here at the periphery of the cortex.

90
00:05:16,300 --> 00:05:18,300
And again, that's called an embolic stroke.

91
00:05:18,300 --> 00:05:22,960
Now let's briefly visit these in the text, then we'll come back to the third cause of ischemic stroke.

92
00:05:23,380 --> 00:05:28,420
And so we say that a thrombotic stroke arises due to rupture of an atherosclerotic plaque.

93
00:05:28,740 --> 00:05:33,040
Remember that atherosclerotic plaques usually develop at branch points of arteries.

94
00:05:33,640 --> 00:05:38,480
Now, this will then result in a pale infarct at the periphery of the cortex, as I've already explained.

95
00:05:38,480 --> 00:05:41,800
And here's a picture just showing you a very, very early infarction.

96
00:05:42,260 --> 00:05:44,420
This is all normal here.

97
00:05:44,720 --> 00:05:47,840
And then you can kind of see here that this is a little bit discolored,

98
00:05:48,180 --> 00:05:50,840
and so this region here is about to undergo infarction.

99
00:05:51,140 --> 00:05:57,900
But again, the point that I'm trying to illustrate here is that you get a pale infarct at the periphery of the cortex.

100
00:05:58,640 --> 00:06:00,600
The second type of stroke would be an embolic stroke.

101
00:06:00,600 --> 00:06:07,460
Here you have a thromboembolus that arises usually from the left side of the heart, classically in the context of atrial fibrillation,

102
00:06:07,780 --> 00:06:11,880
and that goes and lodges into the blood vessel, usually the middle cerebral artery.

103
00:06:12,320 --> 00:06:16,200
This will then result in a hemorrhagic infarct at the periphery of the cortex.

104
00:06:16,620 --> 00:06:23,000
Now, board examiners like you to understand that when you have a thrombotic stroke, for example here,

105
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:26,120
then you are going to get a pale infarction.

106
00:06:26,920 --> 00:06:33,760
And when you have an embolic stroke, for example here, then you are going to get a hemorrhagic infarction.

107
00:06:34,240 --> 00:06:36,440
And you could ask the question, why? What's the difference?

108
00:06:36,800 --> 00:06:42,460
Well, notice that when you have a thrombotic stroke, you rupture the atherosclerotic plaque and you get a thrombus.

109
00:06:42,780 --> 00:06:44,940
Now, of course, the body will try to lyse that thrombus.

110
00:06:45,040 --> 00:06:49,640
However, because the plaque is present and it is ruptured, you'll quickly just reform the thrombus.

111
00:06:49,640 --> 00:06:52,980
So no matter how many times you try to lyse this, it's just going to reform.

112
00:06:53,380 --> 00:06:58,260
And so you won't be able to send blood into this region, and so therefore you'll get a pale stroke.

113
00:06:58,640 --> 00:07:03,560
However, with an embolic stroke, this embolus can easily be lyced.

114
00:07:03,780 --> 00:07:09,260
And once it's lyced, then the blood will be able to return back, and that's what's going to create the hemorrhagic stroke.

115
00:07:09,500 --> 00:07:13,560
So it's high yield to know, again, that in a thrombotic stroke, you get a pale infarction,

116
00:07:13,940 --> 00:07:18,260
but that in an embolic stroke, you get a hemorrhagic infarction.

117
00:07:18,260 --> 00:07:22,360
Now, the third way by which you can get an ischemic stroke is called a lacunar stroke.

118
00:07:22,700 --> 00:07:29,300
And the idea here is that this involves the small blood vessels of the brain that undergo highline arteriolosclerosis.

119
00:07:29,660 --> 00:07:35,660
Of course, highline arteriolosclerosis would occur with benign hypertension or with diabetes.

120
00:07:36,240 --> 00:07:43,240
And when this occurs, this will decrease the blood going through those small blood vessels and will result in a lacunar stroke.

121
00:07:43,240 --> 00:07:48,920
Now, the idea here is that if this is a small blood vessel, and you create highline arteriolosclerosis,

122
00:07:49,080 --> 00:07:51,800
you then thicken the wall, making the lumen more narrow.

123
00:07:52,260 --> 00:07:56,400
This will then result in less blood flow being able to go through this small blood vessel

124
00:07:56,400 --> 00:08:04,100
and will result in a little area of infarction, which is in the region being fed by this small blood vessel.

125
00:08:04,500 --> 00:08:08,240
Now, this then would, of course, be resorbed, producing a small little cystic region.

126
00:08:08,240 --> 00:08:14,220
And they call this a lacunar stroke because it produces small little circular areas that look like lakes.

127
00:08:14,340 --> 00:08:16,020
And the word lacunar actually means lake.

128
00:08:16,260 --> 00:08:18,640
Now, let's use this image to highlight an important point.

129
00:08:18,740 --> 00:08:19,900
Here's the circle of Willis.

130
00:08:20,400 --> 00:08:26,280
And the middle cerebral artery would be running this way and eventually would go out and feed the surface of the cortex.

131
00:08:26,820 --> 00:08:31,520
Now, recall that from the middle cerebral artery, you have these small little tiny branches that come out

132
00:08:31,520 --> 00:08:33,880
and feed the deep structures of the brain.

133
00:08:33,880 --> 00:08:37,540
These little blood vessels are called lenticulostriate vessels.

134
00:08:38,060 --> 00:08:41,920
And lacunar strokes most often occur in these tiny little blood vessels,

135
00:08:42,200 --> 00:08:49,000
which then results in highline arteriolosclerosis and then results in these little lake-like areas of infarction.

136
00:08:49,440 --> 00:08:54,920
And so classically, lacunar strokes will be present in the deep structures of the brain.

137
00:08:55,020 --> 00:09:00,380
These are the deep structures of the brain here and will usually involve the lenticulostriate blood vessels.

138
00:09:00,380 --> 00:09:04,640
And that's what I say here in the text, that it's secondary to highline arteriolosclerosis

139
00:09:04,640 --> 00:09:08,300
and that it most commonly involves the lenticulostriate blood vessels.

140
00:09:08,760 --> 00:09:13,740
Here's a picture of a lacunar stroke just to remind you that you get these small little lake-like areas

141
00:09:13,740 --> 00:09:16,500
within the deep structures of the brain.

142
00:09:17,260 --> 00:09:21,040
When you have an ischemic stroke, this will lead to liquefactive necrosis of the brain.

143
00:09:21,040 --> 00:09:25,500
And one of the earliest findings on microscopy will be that you will see red neurons.

144
00:09:25,900 --> 00:09:28,080
This is particularly high yield for board exams.

145
00:09:28,240 --> 00:09:31,580
They'd like you to know that the earliest change is red neurons

146
00:09:31,580 --> 00:09:34,980
and that it takes about 12 hours for those red neurons to develop.

147
00:09:35,520 --> 00:09:38,380
Now, after the cells die, you'll eventually get inflammation.

148
00:09:38,900 --> 00:09:42,200
And so the inflammation will first be neutrophils and then be macrophages.

149
00:09:42,540 --> 00:09:45,580
But, of course, in the brain, the macrophages are called the microglial cells.

150
00:09:45,900 --> 00:09:47,780
And then, of course, after the first week of inflammation,

151
00:09:47,780 --> 00:09:52,120
you'll then go into a healing phase, which is granulation-like tissue.

152
00:09:52,240 --> 00:09:55,340
It's not exactly granulation tissue, but it's sort of granulation-like.

153
00:09:55,800 --> 00:09:58,620
And then the eventual result, after about a month,

154
00:09:58,680 --> 00:10:02,600
will be that you'll have a fluid-filled cystic space surrounded by gliosis.

155
00:10:02,760 --> 00:10:06,380
And gliosis simply refers to the reactive astrocytes

156
00:10:06,380 --> 00:10:11,280
that will line that cystic space with this gliotic connective tissue.

157
00:10:11,880 --> 00:10:14,420
Now, let me just remind you that the sequence here is very similar

158
00:10:14,420 --> 00:10:16,260
to the sequence that we had within the heart,

159
00:10:16,260 --> 00:10:17,820
if you remember your cardiac lectures.

160
00:10:18,200 --> 00:10:21,940
Remember there I told you one day, one week, one month.

161
00:10:22,280 --> 00:10:25,840
And the idea here is that it takes about a day for the cells to die.

162
00:10:25,940 --> 00:10:27,920
And before that, you would see red neurons.

163
00:10:28,420 --> 00:10:31,860
Once the cells die, you'll have a phase of inflammation

164
00:10:31,860 --> 00:10:33,480
that is from one day to one week.

165
00:10:33,740 --> 00:10:35,440
And it will begin with the neutrophils.

166
00:10:35,740 --> 00:10:38,540
And then, eventually, the microglial cells will come in.

167
00:10:38,760 --> 00:10:42,160
And then from one week to one month, you'll have this granulation tissue.

168
00:10:42,160 --> 00:10:44,400
It's not exactly granulation tissue in the brain,

169
00:10:44,500 --> 00:10:46,360
but it's sort of granulation tissue-like.

170
00:10:46,780 --> 00:10:50,300
But then, eventually, that will result in this cystic space.

171
00:10:51,080 --> 00:10:52,320
So you'll have a cyst.

172
00:10:52,440 --> 00:10:57,180
And, of course, there will be gliosis with astrocytes around that cyst.

173
00:10:57,500 --> 00:11:01,540
And so that's particularly important that the final stage is a gliotic cyst.

174
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:04,720
Now, there's two areas that board examiners like to focus on here.

175
00:11:04,720 --> 00:11:06,800
The first is that you get red neurons.

176
00:11:07,160 --> 00:11:10,220
And so these are the red or pink neurons that are present

177
00:11:10,220 --> 00:11:13,120
as these cells are dying and undergoing apoptosis.

178
00:11:13,620 --> 00:11:17,020
And, of course, this would occur at about 12 hours

179
00:11:17,020 --> 00:11:20,980
and would be present for up to the first 24 hours.

180
00:11:21,340 --> 00:11:22,840
And then the examiners like you to know

181
00:11:22,840 --> 00:11:25,400
that the very end stage is a cystic lesion,

182
00:11:25,560 --> 00:11:28,000
which is surrounded by gliosis.

183
00:11:28,460 --> 00:11:29,960
And, of course, the center is cystic

184
00:11:29,960 --> 00:11:32,140
because it underwent liquefactive necrosis.

185
00:11:32,820 --> 00:11:34,920
Now, the next topic is intracerebral hemorrhage.

186
00:11:34,980 --> 00:11:36,960
But before we discuss this, let's go back.

187
00:11:37,280 --> 00:11:38,880
You have cerebrovascular disease.

188
00:11:38,980 --> 00:11:41,780
And this can be due to ischemia or it can be due to hemorrhage.

189
00:11:42,080 --> 00:11:45,220
When it's due to ischemia, that can be focal or that can be global.

190
00:11:45,700 --> 00:11:47,880
And we've already discussed both focal and global.

191
00:11:48,180 --> 00:11:49,780
So now we're going to discuss hemorrhage.

192
00:11:49,800 --> 00:11:50,940
And there's two types of hemorrhage.

193
00:11:51,220 --> 00:11:54,700
There's intracerebral hemorrhage or hemorrhage into the brain parenchyma.

194
00:11:55,040 --> 00:11:56,760
And then there's subarachnoid hemorrhage,

195
00:11:56,760 --> 00:11:59,740
which is hemorrhage into the subarachnoid space.

196
00:11:59,740 --> 00:12:03,000
And both of these, again, are a type of cerebrovascular disease.

197
00:12:03,480 --> 00:12:04,700
With that background in mind,

198
00:12:04,800 --> 00:12:07,100
intracerebral hemorrhage is bleeding into the brain.

199
00:12:07,460 --> 00:12:10,880
It's classically due to rupture of Charcot-Bouchard microaneurysms,

200
00:12:11,240 --> 00:12:13,020
which is a complication of hypertension.

201
00:12:13,580 --> 00:12:15,860
And the basal ganglia is the most common site.

202
00:12:16,220 --> 00:12:18,780
Let's use this image to clarify some of what we just said.

203
00:12:19,020 --> 00:12:20,760
So here is the circle of Willis.

204
00:12:21,060 --> 00:12:24,360
The middle cerebral artery is coming up and feeding the surface of the cortex.

205
00:12:24,360 --> 00:12:27,640
And recall that you've got these perforating vessels coming here,

206
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:31,060
which are called the lenticulostriate vessels.

207
00:12:31,540 --> 00:12:34,500
Now remember that the lenticulostriate blood vessels can undergo

208
00:12:34,500 --> 00:12:37,520
highline arteriolosclerosis in the face of hypertension.

209
00:12:38,080 --> 00:12:41,720
And that highline arteriolosclerosis will decrease the blood supply through the vessel

210
00:12:41,720 --> 00:12:44,960
and could result in lacunar infarctions, as we've already described.

211
00:12:45,520 --> 00:12:49,020
However, another possibility would be that you could actually get

212
00:12:49,020 --> 00:12:53,020
highline arteriolosclerosis that weakens the wall of these blood vessels,

213
00:12:53,020 --> 00:12:58,040
so that the blood vessels develop something called Charcot-Bouchard microaneurysms.

214
00:12:58,440 --> 00:13:01,020
Now those Charcot-Bouchard microaneurysms can rupture.

215
00:13:01,560 --> 00:13:06,280
And when these rupture, you'll then get bleed within the parenchyma of the brain.

216
00:13:06,780 --> 00:13:09,900
Here's a picture highlighting that the basal ganglia is the most common site.

217
00:13:10,220 --> 00:13:13,820
And again, of course, that's because you've got these lenticulostriate blood vessels

218
00:13:13,820 --> 00:13:16,660
that are feeding the deep structures of the brain.

219
00:13:17,100 --> 00:13:18,600
Moving on to subarachnoid hemorrhage,

220
00:13:18,640 --> 00:13:21,300
this is when you have bleeding into the subarachnoid space.

221
00:13:21,300 --> 00:13:25,140
It presents a sudden headache, quote-unquote the worst headache of my life,

222
00:13:25,440 --> 00:13:26,320
with nuchal rigidity.

223
00:13:26,800 --> 00:13:29,680
Were an LP to be performed, it would show xanthochromia,

224
00:13:30,120 --> 00:13:32,820
which basically refers to a yellow tinge to the CSF

225
00:13:32,820 --> 00:13:35,020
because of bilirubin breakdown products.

226
00:13:35,480 --> 00:13:37,120
Now when you have a subarachnoid hemorrhage,

227
00:13:37,200 --> 00:13:39,240
this is relatively easy on examinations

228
00:13:39,240 --> 00:13:42,320
because you'll see a bleed on the bottom of the brain.

229
00:13:42,660 --> 00:13:46,040
So really, this is the only thing that gives you a bleed on the bottom of the brain.

230
00:13:46,120 --> 00:13:47,580
So they show you the brain upside down

231
00:13:47,580 --> 00:13:50,360
with blood sitting on the basal surface,

232
00:13:50,720 --> 00:13:53,280
and that then would tell you that there's a subarachnoid hemorrhage.

233
00:13:53,720 --> 00:13:56,020
Now very high yield to note that the most frequent cause

234
00:13:56,020 --> 00:13:59,900
of a subarachnoid hemorrhage is rupture of a bary aneurysm.

235
00:14:00,320 --> 00:14:03,940
Recall that bary aneurysms are thin-walled saccular outpouchings

236
00:14:03,940 --> 00:14:07,560
that are most frequently present in the anterior circle of Willis,

237
00:14:07,680 --> 00:14:11,320
particularly at the branch points of the anterior communicating artery.

238
00:14:11,920 --> 00:14:14,060
Very high yield examiners like you to know

239
00:14:14,060 --> 00:14:18,680
that the most frequent location of bary aneurysm is in the anterior circle of Willis

240
00:14:18,680 --> 00:14:22,440
at the branch points of the anterior communicating artery.

241
00:14:22,940 --> 00:14:27,140
Now the idea here is that these bary aneurysms can easily rupture

242
00:14:27,140 --> 00:14:29,540
because they lack a media layer.

243
00:14:30,020 --> 00:14:34,600
Illustrating this, here is a blood vessel with an intima, a media, and an adventitia.

244
00:14:34,700 --> 00:14:37,080
So this is one wall, and here would be another wall

245
00:14:37,080 --> 00:14:40,060
where you have an intima, a media, and an adventitia,

246
00:14:40,300 --> 00:14:41,900
and of course blood is flowing this way.

247
00:14:41,900 --> 00:14:45,440
Now what happens in a bary aneurysm is that when you get branching,

248
00:14:45,860 --> 00:14:48,440
you get turning of the intima at the branch point

249
00:14:48,440 --> 00:14:51,420
and turning of the adventitia at the branch point.

250
00:14:51,780 --> 00:14:54,440
However, the media fails to develop where the branch is.

251
00:14:54,740 --> 00:14:57,960
It would be present as you move further down into the blood vessel,

252
00:14:58,320 --> 00:15:00,200
but it fails to develop at the branch point.

253
00:15:00,580 --> 00:15:02,460
Now when it fails to develop at the branch point,

254
00:15:02,560 --> 00:15:04,920
this then results in a weak wall here,

255
00:15:05,260 --> 00:15:08,860
and so you'd get a saccular outpouching of this blood vessel,

256
00:15:09,020 --> 00:15:10,420
which is called the bary aneurysm,

257
00:15:10,420 --> 00:15:12,920
and of course eventually this can rupture.

258
00:15:13,740 --> 00:15:15,380
And so it's very high yield to note

259
00:15:15,380 --> 00:15:18,880
that the bary aneurysm lacks a media layer,

260
00:15:19,120 --> 00:15:21,400
which then makes it susceptible to rupture.

261
00:15:21,700 --> 00:15:23,800
One last point that often shows up on exams,

262
00:15:24,140 --> 00:15:26,820
and that is bary aneurysms are associated with Marfan syndrome,

263
00:15:27,320 --> 00:15:31,500
and they are also associated with autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease,

264
00:15:31,720 --> 00:15:34,880
which again is the adult form of polycystic kidney disease.

265
00:15:35,380 --> 00:15:37,100
Here's a picture of a bary aneurysm.

266
00:15:37,100 --> 00:15:38,780
Here you've got a normal blood vessel,

267
00:15:39,140 --> 00:15:43,160
and then you can see this saccular outpouching here occurring at a branch point.

268
00:15:43,500 --> 00:15:46,040
And that concludes our section on cerebrovascular disease.

